+
+# JSON Pointer resolution semantics
+
+## Context
+Later features build on `resolvePointer`, and the library must follow RFC 6901 exactly.
+
+## Decision
+- A pointer is invalid unless it is empty or starts with `/`; `~` must be followed by `0` or `1`.
+  Unescaping replaces `~1` before `~0`, so `~01` gives `~1`.
+- Invalid and unresolvable pointers both throw `Error`; the messages say "Invalid JSON Pointer" or
+  "does not resolve", so callers can tell them apart.
+- Array tokens must be `0` or digits without a leading zero, and in range. `-` (the nonexistent element
+  after the last) does not resolve, since it names no value.
+- Object lookup uses own properties only, so `/__proto__`, `/constructor` and similar do not reach
+  inherited members.
+- The URI fragment form (`#/...`) is out of scope and is treated as invalid.
+
+## Consequences
+Callers get strict, standard behaviour with no prototype leakage. A later feature that needs to address
+`-` (for example for appends) must parse pointers itself or extend this module.
